From f7f6ad386e582cc402594df8d82053ddd75e95c7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jared Wyles <jared.wyles@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 10 Nov 2026 17:05:21 +1100
Subject: [PATCH 32/32] skippy: size the unified KV pool and choose its memory
 behaviour from the runtime config

A unified pool was always ctx_size x lane_count cells: every lane holds a
full window at once. With Qwen3-Coder's 131072 window and four lanes that is
524288 cells, 48 GiB of Metal allocation before a request arrives, where
llama-server with -c 32768 -np 4 shares 32768 cells among its slots.

skippy_runtime_config gains four KV controls:

- kv_pool_tokens: the cells of a unified pool. 0 keeps ctx_size x
  lane_count. Any other value is the whole pool, which the lanes share as
  llama-server's slots do with --kv-unified; it must hold at least one
  lane's ctx_size and needs a unified cache, since a split cache gives each
  lane n_ctx / lanes. The MTP draft context takes the target's pool rather
  than recomputing it.
- kv_lazy_clear: FALSE zeroes the whole pool at creation, as llama.cpp does.
- kv_release_pages: FALSE keeps the pages of free chunks.
- kv_row_attention: FALSE keeps ordinary attention over the unified pool, to
  measure the row path against it.

AUTO and TRUE take what each cache supports, so only FALSE changes a cache's
own choice; skippy_apply_kv_memory_config applies the two memory controls to
every attention cache behind a context's memory.

skippy_model_kv_pool_cells reports the pool so the host admits against what
native allocated instead of mirroring the product.

ABI inventory (backward compatibility with older runtimes is not kept; the
Rust mirror changes in lockstep):
- added: uint32_t skippy_model_kv_pool_cells(const struct skippy_model *),
  include/skippy/state.h, src/skippy/state_memory.cpp
- changed: struct skippy_runtime_config gains uint32_t kv_pool_tokens and
  int32_t kv_lazy_clear, kv_release_pages, kv_row_attention after swa_full,
  include/skippy/runtime.h
- removed: none

Bumps the Skippy ABI from 0.1.71 to 0.1.72 at this patch; the model-support
lane's bumps follow from there.

